  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.2. Increased capacity of Member States to strengthen policies and strategies to promote and ensure equal opportunities, participation and treatment between women and men, including equal remuneration for work of equal value</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Georgian Employers Association (GEA) prepared a manual on gender equality for the promotion of equality at the workplace for member enterprises in 2022 with reference to the Manual of the Montenegrin Employers Association (MEF) on Prevention of Discrimination at Work in Montenegro which was translated and adapted to the Georgian context in 2022. Further, as a follow action, GEA adopted a policy paper on Gender Equality in 2022.
The State Concept of Georgia on Gender Equality was first adopted in 2006 and then revised in 2022. This is a multi-sectoral national policy document, which aims to encourage the equal and effective implementation of the rights and opportunities for women and men. The Concept recognizes the principles of gender equality in all spheres of public and social life and provides for the relevant measures for prevention and elimination of all forms of discrimination on the ground of sex as well as for the advancing of gender equality. The Concept determines the State&#x2019;s obligation to provide the relevant legislative and other measures in order to amend the legal framework, which constitutes the discrimination on the ground of sex, and to ensure its implementation. The document encourages the government to integrate gender equality perspectives in the state programmes for economic development and poverty reduction, as well as in the employment policy. It mentions that equal access of women and men to fiscal, credit and production resources is one of the mechanisms for private sector development and for creation of jobs. It also notes that the Georgian Government should pay particular attention to the development of gender-sensitive, social justice-oriented budget both at central and local levels.
More specifically, it provides for the measures in all aspects of life in order to achieve equality; development of the legislation in gender equality; participation in the political and civil lives; economic strengthening and protection of labor rights With the funding support of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Denmark the ILO facilitated the transfer of knowledge and experience from the Montenegrin Employers Association on addressing gender equality and discrimination through organisation of knowledge sharing training between the Montenegrin Employers Association and the Georgian Employers Association (GEA). ILO further provided technical support and guidance in translation and adoption of the A Manual of the Montenegrin Employers Association (MEF) on Prevention of Discrimination at Work in Montenegro into Georgian context. 
As a follow action of the adoption of the policy paper on gender equality, ILO supported GEA in raising awareness among the members of GEA on addressing violence and harassment at the workplace through a training in May 2023.

With the support of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Denmark and within the framework of the &#x201c;Inclusive labour markets for job creation in Georgia&#x201d; Project, ILO provided technical and financial assistance to the constituents through development of evidence-based reports on gender pay gap and facilitation of tripartite discussion on addressing gender pay gap in Georgia which resulted in  further progress at the Government level particularly in the adoption of the Gender Equality Concept of Georgia in December 2022.</narrative>
   </description>
